#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2b57f76-8fab-5b44-80ee-d905e5f9a14f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining How Many Parts Fit Into a Whole"}] :question/id #uuid "4f8f2348-c6ec-5073-9f95-6697173654a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13690" :question/problem "![](images/46/4644be3ee3652c8a52d62e9d849d107e691dbf1f184ea247f02552af108b2e04.png)\n\n \n\nThe $3$ circles above are divided into halves. How many halves are there in total?" :question/worked-solution "Since each part is $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}2}$ of a whole, we need to split each of the $\\color{red}3$ circles (wholes) into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e9/e92a0deb6d9542ca276fba5c7d17c64e45bad265dc8bd8dde2dce4fa166e3e89.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$ parts in total."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed8053e5-f273-56f2-bfb6-294f936d9701" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Model Representing a Division Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "2f739b94-f494-546b-8623-bd17a525a436" :question/math-academy-id "e-13695" :question/problem "What model could represent the division problem below?\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{1}{6} = 18\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the following $\\color{red}3$ wholes:\n\n![](images/23/235e19c4e3231eb22e6d43ccadcd90c5694077ec4ce562e6c4f188cfff77545f.png)\n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}3} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}3$ wholes into $\\color{blue}6$ equal parts, as shown below. This gives us the corresponding model for the given division problem.\n\n![](images/3b/3b3551ad59a9d5abe2bf03e350f373412959293c68a4f059123fafcbabf72936.png)\n\nWe get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}6}= 18$ pieces in total."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e33ab8e-736b-5972-af0d-f7eeabda1148" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Division Problem Represented by a Model"}] :question/id #uuid "dd5b4bc8-110c-5247-b5e6-ee4ce7636a1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13707" :question/problem "Each of the three shapes in the model below represents one whole.\n\n![](images/22/222af9ec253a6624ece646225db926069cd7be3ac416fe846a9216360981f8cb.png)\n\n \n\nFrom left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n![](images/1f/1f6d1040a30647e11e455ec6f50ab4fd41e499b74a47b182495381e1bd92df14.svg)" :question/worked-solution "In the model, there are $\\color{red}3$ shapes. Each shape consists of $4$ pieces that each represent $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a whole. There are $\\color{blue}12$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $\\color{red}3$ wholes into quarters, we will be left with $\\color{blue}12$ pieces.\n\nTherefore, the division problem is:\n![](images/4c/4ca736b0aa98a4104f9a427fe0dad68fbd171b84ce07d02c67a848fe69022724.svg)\n\nSo, the missing numbers are $\\color{red}3$ and $\\color{blue}12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e310e4-b8a2-5327-b4e6-fd71b6783e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "06e730e3-2df3-5d28-afd1-72c34d11cf8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13709" :question/problem "![](images/46/4644be3ee3652c8a52d62e9d849d107e691dbf1f184ea247f02552af108b2e04.png)\n\n \n\nThe model above represents $3$ wholes. Use this model to calculate the value of $3 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate ${\\color{red}3} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}3$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/327833c4954c43c2093cb31a03cef01e66389e7cafc3bb2d4cd174e0f7e64f5d.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 15$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2b57f76-8fab-5b44-80ee-d905e5f9a14f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining How Many Parts Fit Into a Whole"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac751d31-e89b-51ee-a5ce-e348b133b267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac751d31-e89b-51ee-a5ce-e348b133b267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425695} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7fdf3e-1533-56e2-a0e2-8fcb0d845e26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1f85c3b-c75a-57c8-b1c6-0955959346d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-222820" :question/problem "![](images/23/235e19c4e3231eb22e6d43ccadcd90c5694077ec4ce562e6c4f188cfff77545f.png)\n\nIf the $3$ pancakes above are divided into ninths, then there will be{{field-1}}\nninths in total." :question/worked-solution "Since each part is $\\frac{1}{9}$ of a whole, we need to split each of the $3$ pancakes (wholes) into $9$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7e91b7a982a8e5d4f17072484d0315663ae5c5187aefa49e8c756e785d530e.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get $3\\times 9=27$ pieces in total."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2b57f76-8fab-5b44-80ee-d905e5f9a14f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining How Many Parts Fit Into a Whole"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8ba79f3-0b1a-5215-8fff-a21e8694dfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8ba79f3-0b1a-5215-8fff-a21e8694dfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425698} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b33a739-952f-556e-b928-1687b67291d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "231d3968-66d3-5c90-96b8-37d607f95d77" :question/math-academy-id "q-222821" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e817bf1feef0f3534898ff4ef4a342bb8388bf5ef07b3850f84063344832b5a.png)\n\nIf the $3$ dishes above are divided into eighths, then there will be{{field-1}}\neighths in total." :question/worked-solution "Since each part is $\\frac{1}{8}$ of a whole, we need to split each of the $3$ dishes (wholes) into $8$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/55/55be423192cc332d4c8d98223eded4ac3bbed40b36694637912e47a415de9de5.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get $3\\times 8=24$ pieces in total."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e33ab8e-736b-5972-af0d-f7eeabda1148" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Division Problem Represented by a Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bb15768-3c00-57fc-95bd-2dfb30ab2f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bb15768-3c00-57fc-95bd-2dfb30ab2f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425688} :answer-field/id #uuid "343762ec-f000-5e6e-ad75-37a613fc2817" :answer-field/key "field-2" :a